Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.

Question: Is the “ability to consummate” concept subject to Material Adverse Effect (MAE) carveouts as defined below?
Option A: No
Option B: Yes

Merger Agreement Segment:
“Acquisition Material Adverse Disruption” means any event, change, condition or occurrence that, individually or in the aggregate with other such events, would reasonably be expected to have a material adverse effect on (i) the business, assets, liabilities or overall operational results of the Company, or (ii) the ability of the Company to consummate the transactions contemplated by this Agreement. Notwithstanding the foregoing, for the purposes of this definition, the following shall be excluded from any determination of a Material Adverse Disruption:
   (a) any effect attributable solely to general economic downturns, fluctuations in global financial markets, or routine changes in interest rates and currency exchange rates;
   (b) any adverse impact arising directly from the public announcement, pendency, or execution of this Agreement;
   (c) any change arising from modifications in applicable Law, GAAP, or other regulatory mandates enacted after the date hereof, unless such change disproportionately affects the Company relative to similarly situated industry participants;
   (d) any reduction in the efficiency or operational capacity that affects solely the process of consummating the contemplated transaction, absent any further detrimental impact on the Company’s broader business operations.

Based on the language above, determine if the carveout provisions effectively exclude the ability to consummate aspect from being considered a Material Adverse Disruption.
Choices:
